PANEL: Level complete. / CHAPTER 2 · THE MEASUREMENTS · ARCHIVE REVELATION / What the archive kept / 265 story points · 4 documents surfaced from this run / MEMORY / The field cabin / The cabin above the third falls had two bunks, a stove that smoked unless you swore at it, and a shelf of notebooks with the years on their spines in white paint, 1979 to 2031. The notebooks were not there when it went. He had given them away the autumn his knees gave out, which is the only reason I can tell you what was on the shelf. / RECORD · AUGUST 2004 · TOMAS LIND / Gauge 2, reading / Gauge 2, 14:10. Board reads 0.9. Orange from the rock to the bridge: 7.1 s (last August 9.4; 1984 about 13). Second orange eaten by a walker who thought it was being offered. Third orange: 7.0 s. The river is in a hurry and I am not in a position to tell it to stop. Note: buy more oranges. / RECORD · SEPTEMBER 1998 · TOMAS LIND / Field notebook, above the stake line / Walked above the stakes to the moraine at the head of Kessel. There is a lake there now. Not on any map: grey-green, about the size of the football pitch in Riverside, and filling. The moraine holding it looks loose. If it goes, it goes down the Kessel valley and into everything below. Report title, for once not my choice of words: Thirty Stakes and a Lake. Possible they will read that one. / CAPTION / Photograph, 1984 / A young man in a green wool hat holds a survey stake taller than he is, grinning. On the back, in pencil: Stake 1. It will see me out. / The Red Canyon / The measurements were never secret. They were in reports with plain titles, filed in the proper places, sent to the people whose job it was to read them. Some of those people did. One wrote "very good" in the margin and passed it on. The water in the canyon got faster every decade, and the oranges proved it, and the board on the bridge proved it, and the canyon did not wait for anyone to agree. / From the archive · authored for this outcome / The run, as recorded / CO