PANEL: Level complete. / CHAPTER 4 · THE PEOPLE · ARCHIVE REVELATION / What the archive kept / 2,900 story points · 4 documents surfaced from this run / MEMORY / No. 14 / The chalk mark on No. 14 was a circle with a line through it: empty. I drew it myself, at ten past six, and then stood there holding the chalk because there was nothing left on my list. Inside: the calendar, a piano nobody had tuned since 2020, eleven boxes of this archive I could not carry. The water does not read chalk. I have kept the chalk. / LETTER · MARCH 2038 · GIL HARROW / Letter from Gil Harrow / Dear Miss V., you asked me at the meeting whether I would say on the record why the spillway was deferred in 2009 and 2016. I said I would think about it. I have. It was deferred because the national inspectors called the dam satisfactory, because the harbour needed dredging, because the school roof leaked, and because I believed we had time. Three of those were true. Yours, as ever, Gil Harrow / RECORD · 2017 · RIVERSIDE TOWN COUNCIL / Riverside flood levy, result / Question: should the town raise a yearly charge on every household to build a flood wall for the lower town and raise the dam spillway? Yes: 1,402. No: 1,618. Spoilt: 9. On the back of one spoilt paper, in blue biro: I would if I could. The wall was built in 2036 with national money. It was built to the 2009 design. / MEMORY / Next door / Mrs Pell brought shortbread to every wardens' meeting for three years and told everyone she had voted against the levy, so nobody would have to wonder. She had a roof to fix that year; I have her receipt for it too. She came to every drill. She knocked on every door on her list. On the twelfth she chalked her own door before I could, and took the shortbread tin. / The Chalk / You went through No. 14 Church Lane and the Pells' house next door, then the Riverside dam gave way. Eight houses went. I had marked No. 14 with a circle crossed by a line: empty. Inside were the calendar, a piano nobody had tuned in years, and the archive boxes. I was still holding the chalk. The wardens had tested it in rain. It did not help against water. / Gil Harrow wrote that the spillway had been deferred because the national inspectors called the dam satisfactory, the harbour needed dredging, the school roof leaked, and he believed there was time. At the levy vote, No had more votes than Yes. The wall came later, built to the old design. I kept the chalk. / Written by the Archivist · reviewed by the Historian · checked against your run / How this was made / The run, as recorded / CONTINUE TO CHAPTER 5 / REPLAY CHAPT
